Subject: Report a class's conversion functions through `ParseCallbacks`

bindgen parses no member from a conversion function. libclang reports one as a
`CXCursor_ConversionFunction`, and the visitor in `CompInfo::from_ty` lists that
cursor among the ones it passes over, so such a member reaches no IR, no
callback and no output. Nothing bindgen generates distinguishes a class which
declares one from a class which declares none.

That silence costs more than the count of such classes suggests. A class whose
only reader is a conversion function - a distance wrapper which converts to an
integer, a handle which converts to a string - is generated as a type with no
way to read it at all, and nothing in the bindings says why. The reader is left
to find the C++ declaration and work out that bindgen never saw it.

`--report-conversion-functions` keeps what C++ said about each of them and
reports it through `denote_conversion_function`: what the function converts to,
the constness, and the access. Nothing is generated for one with the option on
either. Whether bindgen *should* generate them is a separate question and a
larger one - a conversion function's name is `operator` followed by a type, so
every generated name would have to be invented from that type and kept stable
as the type's spelling changes - and this is only about not being silent while
that question is open.

The target type comes from `clang_getCursorResultType` rather than from the
cursor's spelling. The spelling prints the type through a policy of libclang's
choosing and puts the word `operator` in front of it, which a consumer would
then have to take back off; the result type is the same type without either
step. It is reported qualified - `Type::qualified_spelling` rather than
`Type::spelling` - because a consumer which reads `string` cannot tell which
`string` it was handed, and the name is there to be looked up. It is the type as
the *declaration* spells it, so for a class template `operator T` reports `T`:
nothing here instantiates anything, and a consumer which cares reads the class's
template parameters beside it.

A conversion function template is not reported, where a conversion function of
any other kind now is. libclang gives one a `CXCursor_FunctionTemplate`, which
`--report-member-function-templates` already passes over for the same reason it
would be useless here: libclang spells one `operator type-parameter-0-0` rather
than `operator T`, so the target type - the whole of what distinguishes one
conversion function from another - does not survive.

The report is made from `CompInfo::codegen`, beside the reports every class
makes, and is subject to `--generate=methods` as any other member function is.
There is no per-member blocklist to consult, a conversion function never
becoming an item for a blocklist to match, and a blocklisted class does not
reach codegen at all.

Opt-in, because a report made unconditionally would be a new one for every
consumer which already implements `ParseCallbacks`. With the option off nothing
changes at all.

